Ladybird paints the root background’s color once over the canvas: in
the root element’s own background, or, when the root is hidden or
transformed, in the canvas fill. Blink paints it once, from the view, in
ViewPainter::PaintRootElementGroup(). WebKit paints it from the view in
RenderView::paintBoxDecorations(), and RenderBox treats the document
element’s background as special, rather than painting it again. Gecko
paints it once, from the canvas frame, with the style it gets from
nsCSSRendering::FindRootFrameBackground().

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, a root with
a 40% blue background comes out rgb(153, 153, 255) in all three — with
a transform, a blur filter, visibility: hidden, a propagated body
background, an image layer, or background-clip, as it now does in
Ladybird. They differ with the root’s opacity: Chrome applies it to the
canvas background (rgb(204, 204, 255)), and Firefox and WebKit don’t
(rgb(153, 153, 255)). And with filter: invert(1), Chrome inverts the
color alone, while Firefox and WebKit invert the composited canvas.
Ladybird now matches Chrome in both.
